{
  // Locks which SDK builds this repository. Without this file, whichever SDK a machine
  // happens to carry compiles the binary a user is asked to run as Administrator, and
  // "it builds on my machine" stops being evidence of anything.
  //
  // rollForward is the whole decision, and both extremes are wrong here:
  //
  //   "disable" demands 10.0.302 exactly; "patch" is looser than it sounds -- it accepts a
  //   later patch of this feature band (10.0.3xx) but nothing beyond it. Both fail the same
  //   day for the same reason: the GitHub runner images refresh their preinstalled SDK every
  //   few weeks, setup-dotnet is asked for the '10.0.x' band, and the first time that band
  //   resolves to 10.0.4xx every job stops with "compatible SDK not found" -- on a commit
  //   that changed nothing. A lock that has to be edited to keep a correct build green is
  //   one that gets deleted after the second time.
  //
  //   "latestMajor" would accept a .NET 11 SDK, which is to say it would lock nothing: it
  //   is precisely a major this code has never been compiled with that must not silently
  //   produce the deliverable.
  //
  // "latestFeature" is the range in between: a floor at 10.0.302 -- the SDK this repository
  // is developed and released with -- and a ceiling at the end of 10.0, since it rolls
  // forward across feature bands but never across major.minor.
  //
  // That range is exactly the '10.0.x' band .github/workflows/{ci,release}.yml ask
  // setup-dotnet for. The difference is who enforces it: the band is a request made to one
  // installer on one runner, this file is checked by MSBuild on every machine, including a
  // workstation and a fork's self-hosted runner that never read the workflow.
  // BuildChainParityTests holds the two spellings together, because they are two
  // hand-written strings in two files that no tool relates to one another.
  //
  // Comments are legal here: the SDK resolver and setup-dotnet (which parses with JSON5)
  // both accept them. Verified, not assumed -- "dotnet --version" resolves 10.0.302 with
  // this header in place.
  "sdk": {
    "version": "10.0.302",
    "rollForward": "latestFeature"
  }
}
